nts. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement VI is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-2469" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62bff426ce2a3d3733958909ae26160db95e94c280c7f7746696465f5547d25c"} {"question" "q-2469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-2469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, III, and IV"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-2469" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and IV only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-22079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)={x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-22079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=6x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is zero, so we should apply the first derivative test.\n\nLet's evaluate the first derivative at auxiliary points $\\pm 1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=0,$ $f(x)$ has no relative extrema.\n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)=0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-22079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62bff426ce2a3d3733958909ae26160db95e94c280c7f7746696465f5547d25c"} {"question" "q-22079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-22079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "q-22079" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "2af71a7e8d06221fb61ec24c3b9b09ca5d56d1028308c1630e697ab822f4feb1"} {"question" "e-3744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3744.json" "file_sha256" "ee17bd7030b3b511f10fe17bb2baaf4f385d32e8236cf6b0a1746ad20b833d84"} {"question" "e-3744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3744.json" "file_sha256" "ee17bd7030b3b511f10fe17bb2baaf4f385d32e8236cf6b0a1746ad20b833d84"} {"question" "e-3743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3743.json" "file_sha256" "2408a90c9c5759e888fdec1621891586d532b8cda3c26befcf3e65418487e564"} {"question" "e-3743" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/example-3743.json" "file_sha256" "2408a90c9c5759e888fdec1621891586d532b8cda3c26befcf3e65418487e564"}] "usage" {"e-3743" [] "q-2459" [] "e-3744" []} "basis" 1228 "retractions" [[17592186064555 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor the function $f(x) = 6x^{2} - 4x^{3}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{″}(0) > 0$\n2. $x = 0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n3. $f^{″}(1) < 0$\n4. $x = 1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$"] [17592186063965 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x$. Given that $f'(5) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f''(5) > 0$\n2. $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n3. $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$"] [17592186063965 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x)$. We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3-6x^2-15x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0$. Indeed, we can see that\n\n$$\nf'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(5) &= 6(5) - 12 \\\\\n&= 30 - 12 \\\\\n&= 18 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186063964 :question/problem "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point."] [17592186063964 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 16 x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 16x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2)(x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2$.\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x)$, we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-2) &= 12(-2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 16 =-16 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0$, so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2$, so these are relative minima."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186061842 17592186061843 17592186061844 17592186061845 17592186061846] "immutable_fields" [[17592186061841 :answer-field/correct] [17592186061841 :answer-field/id] [17592186061841 :answer-field/key] [17592186061841 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
